; truth-arrival.fk — boolean, then fuzzy, then the escape from both:
; a truth that can be ABSENT, and that ARRIVES.
;
; Urs: build a core boolean and fuzzy logic reasoning framework, then escape
; boolean logic by bringing nothing and time into the mix.
;
; THE LADDER, EACH RUNG THE ONE BELOW PLUS ONE THING
;
;   1. BOOLEAN   values {0, 1}. Total and timeless: every proposition has a
;                value, and it has it now.
;   2. FUZZY     values [0, 1]. Still total, still timeless. The interval is
;                new; the totality is not.
;   3. ARRIVAL   values [0, 1] union NOTHING, indexed by time. Truth becomes
;                PARTIAL (a proposition may have no value at all) and TEMPORAL
;                (absence can resolve; it is not a verdict).
;
; Rung 3 is the escape, and it escapes rung 2 as much as rung 1 — which is the
; part worth being careful about.
;
; WHY FUZZY IS NOT ALREADY THE ESCAPE
;   The tempting mistake is to read 0.5 as "I don't know". It is not. 0.5 is a
;   CLAIM — "half true", a definite value, as committed as 0 or 1. Fuzzy logic
;   is total: it has no way to decline. Ask it anything and it answers with a
;   degree, because a degree is all it has.
;
;   `nothing` is the absence of a claim, and the band proves it is no degree in
;   disguise: for EVERY sampled mu, `and(true, mu)` is known, while
;   `and(true, nothing)` is not. No point on the interval behaves like the
;   absence. (ta-not-a-degree? below sweeps this rather than asserting it.)
;
; GROUND
;   axiom-1 states — 0, 1, nothing; nothing is first-class, the ground, not a
;                    missing 0. And: timeout IS nothing. That second clause is
;                    the whole of rung 3 — it says absence is what time
;                    produces, so a logic with nothing in it is already a logic
;                    with time in it, whether or not it admits so.
;   axiom-5 offer  — the ack alphabet {nothing, 0, 1, node}. See THE MERGE.
;
; THE KLEENE EXTENSION, DERIVED — NOT TABLED
;   Most three-valued logics write their connectives as a 3x3 table and ask you
;   to accept it. This cell derives them from ONE rule:
;
;     a result is known exactly when it is the SAME for every way the absent
;     value could resolve.
;
;   Since min / max / complement are monotone in each argument, the value over
;   an absent input ranges between its two endpoints, so it is constant exactly
;   when those endpoints agree. Two probes decide it. ta-ext1 / ta-ext2 do that
;   once, and every connective inherits the behaviour for free — add a new
;   monotone connective and it is Kleene-correct without another line.
;
;   The table that falls out is the interesting part:
;
;     and(0, nothing)    = 0        answerable WITHOUT WAITING
;     and(1, nothing)    = nothing  waits
;     or (1, nothing)    = 1        answerable without waiting
;     or (0, nothing)    = nothing  waits
;     not(nothing)       = nothing
;     and(0.3, nothing)  = nothing  a degree does not absorb; only the poles do
;
; THE ESCAPE, STATED AS THE ONE THING NEITHER RUNG BELOW CAN DO
;   SOME ANSWERS ARRIVE BEFORE THEIR INPUTS.
;
;   `and(false, never-arrives)` is known false, now, forever, on an input that
;   never comes. Boolean cannot express it and neither can fuzzy, because both
;   demand a total valuation before they will speak — every input needs a
;   value for the expression to have one. Rung 3 drops totality, and what it
;   buys with that is the ability to finish early. Waiting becomes a thing the
;   logic can tell you that you DON'T have to do.
;
;   And the guarantee that makes it safe is a theorem, not a hope: because the
;   derived answer is the answer that holds for EVERY resolution, any actual
;   future confirms it. So truth never un-resolves — once known, known, and
;   equal, at every later time. ta-monotone? walks a time sweep and checks it.
;
; ONE CLAIM, THREE DIFFERENT DEATHS
;   Excluded middle (p or not p = true) is the cleanest place to see the three
;   rungs pull apart, and each breaks it in its own way:
;
;     boolean   or(1, not 1)             = 1        holds
;     fuzzy     or(0.5, not 0.5)         = 0.5      fails BY DEGREE   (a weaker truth)
;     arrival   or(nothing, not nothing) = nothing  fails BY ABSENCE  (no truth at all)
;
;   Fuzzy weakens it. Nothing withdraws it. They are not the same failure,
;   and the band computes all three rather than narrating them.
;
; THE MERGE, AND WHAT IT FORCES
;   These values are axiom-5's ack alphabet with the interval filled in:
;   ta-false is the 0 arm, ta-true the 1 arm, ta-nothing the nothing arm. But
;   axiom-5 has NO fuzzy arm — its terminal acks are exactly axiom-1's three
;   states, and 0.3 is not one of them. So a degree cannot cross a membrane as
;   a terminal ack at all: it has to ride as a NODE.
;
;   That is not a limitation to route around, it is the protocol being right. A
;   degree is a composed thing — it has a scale, a source, a reading — and
;   axiom-2 says composed things are cells. Forcing partial truth to travel as
;   a cell keeps it content-addressed and inspectable instead of an opaque
;   number that lost its provenance in transit. ta-ack-arm computes the arm,
;   and the band checks that the interior lands on NODE.
;
; Degrees are per-mille integers 0..1000 — exact under the kernel's integer
; comparison, no float promotion anywhere on this surface.
;
; Proof: form-stdlib/tests/truth-arrival-band.fk → four-way (Go/Rust/TS/fkwu).
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk form-stdlib/offer-ack-core.fk

(do
    ;; ── the carrier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;; (0 0) is absence; (1 mu) is presence at degree mu.
    (defn ta-nothing () (list 0 0))
    (defn ta-deg (mu) (list 1 mu))
    (defn ta-known? (v) (nth v 0))
    (defn ta-mu (v) (nth v 1))
    (defn ta-unit () 1000)
    (defn ta-false () (ta-deg 0))
    (defn ta-true  () (ta-deg (ta-unit)))
    (defn ta-eq? (a b)
        (if (eq (ta-known? a) (ta-known? b))
            (if (eq (ta-known? a) 0) 1 (if (eq (ta-mu a) (ta-mu b)) 1 0))
            0))

    ;; ── rung 1 + 2: the connectives on degrees (Zadeh / Godel) ──────
    ;; Restricted to {0, 1000} these ARE the classical tables; the band
    ;; walks all eight rows rather than trusting the claim.
    (defn ta-min (a b) (if (lt a b) a b))
    (defn ta-max (a b) (if (gt a b) a b))
    (defn ta-comp (a) (sub (ta-unit) a))

    ;; ── rung 3: the derived extension over absence ──────────────────
    ;; Known exactly when both probes agree — i.e. when the answer does not
    ;; depend on how the absent input resolves.
    (defn ta-agree (x y) (if (eq x y) (ta-deg x) (ta-nothing)))
    (defn ta-agree4 (a b c d)
        (if (eq a b)
            (if (eq b c) (if (eq c d) (ta-deg a) (ta-nothing)) (ta-nothing))
            (ta-nothing)))

    (defn ta-ext1 (op a)
        (if (eq (ta-known? a) 1) (ta-deg (op (ta-mu a)))
            (ta-agree (op 0) (op (ta-unit)))))

    (defn ta-ext2 (op a b)
        (if (eq (ta-known? a) 1)
            (if (eq (ta-known? b) 1) (ta-deg (op (ta-mu a) (ta-mu b)))
                (ta-agree (op (ta-mu a) 0) (op (ta-mu a) (ta-unit))))
            (if (eq (ta-known? b) 1)
                (ta-agree (op 0 (ta-mu b)) (op (ta-unit) (ta-mu b)))
                (ta-agree4 (op 0 0) (op 0 (ta-unit))
                           (op (ta-unit) 0) (op (ta-unit) (ta-unit))))))

    ;; the three connectives — none carries its own absence logic
    (defn ta-and (a b) (ta-ext2 ta-min a b))
    (defn ta-or  (a b) (ta-ext2 ta-max a b))
    (defn ta-not (a)   (ta-ext1 ta-comp a))

    ;; ── time: a signal is a degree that arrives ─────────────────────
    ;; Before its arrival it is nothing — not false. That distinction is the
    ;; whole reason rung 3 exists: "not yet" and "no" are different answers,
    ;; and a logic that cannot tell them apart will report one as the other.
    (defn ta-never () 999999)
    (defn ta-sig (arrival mu) (list arrival mu))
    (defn ta-arrival (s) (nth s 0))
    (defn ta-at (s t)
        (if (ge t (ta-arrival s)) (ta-deg (nth s 1)) (ta-nothing)))
    (defn ta-and-at (s1 s2 t) (ta-and (ta-at s1 t) (ta-at s2 t)))
    (defn ta-or-at  (s1 s2 t) (ta-or  (ta-at s1 t) (ta-at s2 t)))

    ;; ── the theorems, walked rather than asserted ───────────────────
    ;; T1 truth never un-resolves: once the expression is known at some t, it
    ;; is known and EQUAL at every later t. Holds by construction — the derived
    ;; answer is the one true of every resolution, so a real future confirms it
    ;; — and the sweep is what makes the construction answerable.
    (defn ta-mono-loop (s1 s2 t tmax first)
        (if (gt t tmax) 1
            (do (let v (ta-and-at s1 s2 t))
                (if (eq (ta-known? first) 0)
                    (ta-mono-loop s1 s2 (add t 1) tmax v)
                    (if (eq (ta-known? v) 0) 0
                        (if (eq (ta-eq? v first) 1)
                            (ta-mono-loop s1 s2 (add t 1) tmax first)
                            0))))))
    (defn ta-monotone? (s1 s2 tmax) (ta-mono-loop s1 s2 0 tmax (ta-nothing)))

    ;; T2 the early answer: known now, on an input that never arrives.
    (defn ta-early-answer? ()
        (do (let v (ta-and-at (ta-sig 0 0) (ta-sig (ta-never) (ta-unit)) 0))
            (if (eq (ta-known? v) 1) (if (eq (ta-mu v) 0) 1 0) 0)))

    ;; T3 nothing is no degree in disguise: sweep the interval and find that
    ;; every mu leaves and(true, mu) KNOWN, while and(true, nothing) is not.
    (defn ta-sweep-known (mu step)
        (if (gt mu (ta-unit)) 1
            (if (eq (ta-known? (ta-and (ta-true) (ta-deg mu))) 1)
                (ta-sweep-known (add mu step) step)
                0)))
    (defn ta-not-a-degree? ()
        (if (eq (ta-sweep-known 0 50) 1)
            (if (eq (ta-known? (ta-and (ta-true) (ta-nothing))) 0) 1 0)
            0))

    ;; ── the classical tables, walked on the boolean sub-lattice ─────
    (defn ta-bool-and-ok? ()
        (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-and (ta-false) (ta-false))) 0)
            (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-and (ta-false) (ta-true))) 0)
                (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-and (ta-true) (ta-false))) 0)
                    (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-and (ta-true) (ta-true))) (ta-unit)) 1 0) 0) 0) 0))
    (defn ta-bool-or-ok? ()
        (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-or (ta-false) (ta-false))) 0)
            (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-or (ta-false) (ta-true))) (ta-unit))
                (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-or (ta-true) (ta-false))) (ta-unit))
                    (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-or (ta-true) (ta-true))) (ta-unit)) 1 0) 0) 0) 0))
    (defn ta-bool-not-ok? ()
        (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-not (ta-false))) (ta-unit))
            (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-not (ta-true))) 0) 1 0) 0))
    (defn ta-boolean-recovered? ()
        (if (eq (ta-bool-and-ok?) 1)
            (if (eq (ta-bool-or-ok?) 1)
                (if (eq (ta-bool-not-ok?) 1) 1 0) 0) 0))

    ;; de Morgan on the fuzzy interior — the interval keeps the duality even
    ;; where it loses excluded middle
    (defn ta-demorgan-at? (a b)
        (if (eq (ta-eq? (ta-not (ta-and (ta-deg a) (ta-deg b)))
                        (ta-or (ta-not (ta-deg a)) (ta-not (ta-deg b)))) 1) 1 0))
    (defn ta-demorgan? ()
        (if (eq (ta-demorgan-at? 700 300) 1)
            (if (eq (ta-demorgan-at? 125 875) 1)
                (if (eq (ta-demorgan-at? 500 500) 1) 1 0) 0) 0))

    ;; ── one claim, three deaths ───────────────────────────────────────
    (defn ta-lem (v) (ta-or v (ta-not v)))
    ;; boolean: holds
    (defn ta-lem-boolean-holds? ()
        (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-lem (ta-true))) (ta-unit))
            (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-lem (ta-false))) (ta-unit)) 1 0) 0))
    ;; fuzzy: fails BY DEGREE — a value, just not the top one
    (defn ta-lem-fuzzy-fails-by-degree? ()
        (do (let v (ta-lem (ta-deg 500)))
            (if (eq (ta-known? v) 1)
                (if (eq (ta-mu v) 500) 1 0)
                0)))
    ;; arrival: fails BY ABSENCE — not a weaker truth, no truth
    (defn ta-lem-nothing-fails-by-absence? ()
        (if (eq (ta-known? (ta-lem (ta-nothing))) 0) 1 0))

    ;; ── the merge with axiom-5's ack alphabet ───────────────────────
    ;; 0 = nothing, 1 = zero, 2 = one, 3 = node — oac's own tag vocabulary.
    ;; The interior has no terminal arm, so it lands on NODE by construction.
    (defn ta-ack-arm (v)
        (if (eq (ta-known? v) 0) (oac-tag-nothing)
            (if (eq (ta-mu v) 0) (oac-tag-zero)
                (if (eq (ta-mu v) (ta-unit)) (oac-tag-one)
                    (oac-tag-node)))))
    (defn ta-merge-ok? ()
        (if (eq (ta-ack-arm (ta-nothing)) (oac-tag-nothing))
            (if (eq (ta-ack-arm (ta-false)) (oac-tag-zero))
                (if (eq (ta-ack-arm (ta-true)) (oac-tag-one))
                    (if (eq (ta-ack-arm (ta-deg 300)) (oac-tag-node))
                        (if (eq (ta-ack-arm (ta-deg 999)) (oac-tag-node)) 1 0) 0) 0) 0) 0))

    ;; ── the band's fourteen readings ────────────────────────────────
    (defn ta-check ()
        (add (if (eq (ta-boolean-recovered?) 1) 1 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-and (ta-deg 700) (ta-deg 300))) 300) 10 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-mu (ta-or (ta-deg 700) (ta-deg 300))) 700) 100 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-demorgan?) 1) 1000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-lem-boolean-holds?) 1) 10000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-lem-fuzzy-fails-by-degree?) 1) 100000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-lem-nothing-fails-by-absence?) 1) 1000000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-eq? (ta-and (ta-false) (ta-nothing)) (ta-false)) 1) 10000000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-known? (ta-and (ta-true) (ta-nothing))) 0) 100000000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-eq? (ta-or (ta-true) (ta-nothing)) (ta-true)) 1)
                 (if (eq (ta-known? (ta-or (ta-false) (ta-nothing))) 0) 1000000000 0) 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-known? (ta-and (ta-deg 300) (ta-nothing))) 0) 10000000000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-not-a-degree?) 1) 100000000000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-early-answer?) 1) 1000000000000 0)
        (add (if (eq (ta-monotone? (ta-sig 3 1000) (ta-sig 7 400) 12) 1)
                 (if (eq (ta-monotone? (ta-sig 0 0) (ta-sig (ta-never) 1000) 12) 1)
                     (if (eq (ta-merge-ok?) 1) 10000000000000 0) 0) 0)
             0)))))))))))))))

    0)
